For each character in the string, we can either include it in a subsequence or not. If we include it, we can form a new subsequence by adding it to all the subsequences that end with the previous character. If we don't include it, we can form a new subsequence by adding it to all the subsequences that end with the previous character.

We can use dynamic programming to solve this problem. Let dp[i][j] be the number of distinct subsequences of the first i characters of the string that end with the jth character. Then, dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] + dp[i-1][j-1] if the i-th character is equal to the j-th character, and dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j] otherwise.

The final answer is the sum of all dp[n][j] for j from 1 to n, where n is the length of the string.

